  "evaluation": "All three correctly classify the disagreement as genuine: both assess vitamin D status and depressive symptoms, and neither provides evidence that the null result was underpowered or measured a different construct. C is strongest because it foregrounds the case-control versus confounder-adjusted cohort designs, a plausible explanation for the differing associations, while retaining population and outcome-measure differences. A and B also reasonably prioritize the substantial population difference; A is slightly more specific about the settings and depression instruments.",
  "ranking": ["C", "A", "B"]
}
